# Line-ending policy (single source of truth for every platform).
#
# Why this file exists: the project is edited on Windows and run on WSL2
# Ubuntu. With no .gitattributes and core.autocrlf=true on the Windows side,
# git synthesized CRLF on checkout there while the same files are LF on Linux,
# so `git status` reported a pile of files as "modified" that had not changed
# by a single byte (`git hash-object <f>` == `git rev-parse HEAD:<f>`). That
# noise makes a real change hard to spot, which is exactly what the project's
# fail-loud discipline exists to prevent.
#
# The repository already stores LF (verified: zero 0x0d bytes in the tracked
# blobs), so `eol=lf` states the existing truth rather than rewriting history.
# Nothing is normalized on the way in or out, and the working tree stops
# disagreeing with the index.

# Default: text files are stored as LF and checked out as LF everywhere, so the
# Windows and Ubuntu checkouts of the same commit are byte-identical.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF. A CRLF shebang (`#!/bin/sh\r`) makes the kernel
# look for a interpreter literally named "sh\r" and the script fails to run on
# the Linux side. These are executed directly in the reference environment.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-only launchers must stay CRLF - cmd.exe tolerates a bare LF poorly
# and the vendored jadx/apktool wrappers are expected to look native there.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Source, config and docs: LF, consistent with the rest of the tree.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.ya?ml  text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.ini    text eol=lf
*.cfg    text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf

# Binary - never touch these. An APK re-encoded as text is a corrupt APK, and
# the .so files inside are ELF images, not text.
*.apk    binary
*.jar    binary
*.zip    binary
*.so     binary
*.dex    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
